name o2-10-exercise-two-1
timeout 500
time 1984-06-16T20:00:00Z

# CAMPAIGN O2, SORTIE 10 OF 10 - PVO INTERCEPT EXERCISE: GROUND CONTROL IN ITS PURE FORM.
# THE EXERCISE, WAVE TWO, AT NIGHT: THE SAME FOUR AIRCRAFT WITH WHAT WAVE ONE LEFT THEM.
# Spec, anchor and sources: doc/campaigns/o2-pvo-intercept.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
# CONTROL: none. CHAIN TERMINUS - it inherits `pvo1`..`pvo4` and the `pvo*` complex from o2-09.
#
# THE CHAIN, AND WHY IT IS TWO WAVES AND NOT THREE. Sorties 09 and 10 are one roster (`pvo1`..`pvo4`)
# and one defended complex (`pvoews`, `pvocp`, `pvoveh`) flown twice, with `carry units ground stores`
# NOT narrowed: wave two flies with what wave one left. The carry sits here and nowhere else, because
# this is the only place the campaign has a QUESTION about it - IS THE CONTROLLER WORTH THE SAME TO A
# FORCE THAT HAS ALREADY SPENT ITS MAGAZINES AND LOST AIRCRAFT? Ten slots hold one baseline, four
# single-lever variants, one control pair plus its own control run, and this chain; a third wave did not
# fit. WHAT THAT COSTS IS STATED: two points give a DIFFERENCE, never a TREND, so this campaign cannot
# show a force-state curve and does not claim one.
#
# THE CORRIDOR FRIENDLY IS THE T1 ANCHOR ARRIVING AS A UNIT. Mitronin (Warsaw Pact journal 12/1976, read
# in this round) writes that corridors are established through the air-defence zone to guarantee the
# safety of one's own flights, that the enemy may discover and exploit them, and that they must be
# covered by units WITH IDENTIFICATION MEANS. Each wave therefore carries one aircraft on OUR team,
# transponder on, flying inside the raid's piece of sky. It has `objective survive`, its loss is covered
# by nobody, and a fratricide therefore ENDS THE RUN AS A FAIL. That is not a hazard of the design; it is
# the measurement.
#
# THE ARENA, and every coordinate in it is [SET]. An exercise has no battlefield, so this campaign flies
# over the Soviet air-defence firing range at Ashuluk, Astrakhan oblast [T4], taken as ~47.30 N 47.90 E
# and NOT improved on. Flown under `--elev const` on a 0 m datum and with NO TERRAIN MASKING (gap C4).
#   the defended complex   a command post (target_hard) and a vehicle park (target_soft) - the objects
#                          the vector exists to protect
#   the early-warning set  a P-18, `set emcon free`, deployed forward of the complex on the approach
#                          axis. GAP C1 IS CLOSED AND THIS CAMPAIGN RE-CHECKED IT: the spec still says
#                          "target_soft only, the EW radar that generates the vector is inert". It is
#                          not inert any more - it radiates, it is heard by the intruder's RWR as
#                          `kind=surface-search` from t = 0.1, it builds its own tracks, and it can be
#                          killed. Every ground object in this campaign is OURS and none is a target
# Scale at this latitude: 1 deg of longitude = 75 490 m, 1 deg of latitude = 111 132 m [DERIVED].
#
# TEMPORAL HONESTY AND ITS DIRECTION. The PVO's own subject was a large, slow, high, unmanoeuvring
# aircraft - a bomber or an ELINT platform - and FlightBox has neither as a flyable module (gap C7: the
# catalogue's `tu95` row is a KINEMATIC MOVER, not an airframe). The intruder here is an F-16: faster,
# smaller and far harder to find than the aircraft the doctrine was written against. THE SUBSTITUTION
# MAKES THE INTERCEPT HARDER THAN HISTORY, so a FlightBox result in which the loop still closes says
# something WEAKER than the doctrine claimed, and one in which it does not close says nothing about it.
#
# AND THE CATALOGUE IS NOT A WAY ROUND IT. Four `air` rows are ACCEPTED, and ACCEPTED in
# doc/modules/air/flight-model-recipe.md means ACCEPTED AS A FLIGHT MODEL - the promotion gate measures
# the DECK and nothing else. It measures no weapon, no sensor and no engagement, and four of the ten
# armed rows cannot employ anything at all. A campaign citing a catalogue row as a COMBATANT would be
# citing something no gate has ever checked, so this campaign cites none: every aircraft in all ten
# files is one of the two flyable modules.
#
# THE STRUCTURAL DISCLOSURE, repeated in all ten files because a reader who skips it reads every result
# backwards:
#   1. THE CONTROLLER IS A STRING (gap C6). `set brief_gci` is fixed before the run: nothing re-vectors,
#      nothing goes silent mid-intercept, nothing is wrong halfway through. Every wrong brief in this
#      campaign is wrong FROM THE START, which is the easy case, and the campaign says so.
#   2. THE PILOT CANNOT DISAGREE WITH HIS BRIEF, AND WHAT LOOKS LIKE A RECOVERY IS A DEAD-BAND ACCIDENT.
#      `FBMig29Pilot` types what it was given and has no path to re-enter a call it has reason to doubt.
#      `FBPilot`'s own uncued search law does keep posting an elevation, but only when ITS OWN wanted
#      angle has moved more than `kInterceptElDeadDeg` = 2.0 deg from ITS OWN last command - and that
#      cache is per aircraft. MEASURED in sortie 02: the wingman's cache sat at 0.0, drifted past the
#      band at t = 55.9 and pulled the antenna back down; the leader's sat at -2.0 from t = 3.5, never
#      drifted, and never looked at the raid again. One aircraft of two recovered, 28 s late, and not
#      because anybody doubted the controller. So 02 and 03 measure the cost of a wrong entry AND the
#      absence of any deliberate recovery, and the two cannot be separated inside one file.
#   3. THE GCI ELEVATION ENTRY IS A WORLD-FRAME ANGLE IN A BODY-FRAME COMMAND (doc/pilot.md 2.15, found
#      by O5 and STILL OPEN on the tree this campaign was flown against). Every interceptor in all ten
#      files is therefore laid out in LEVEL flight, and what that costs is measured in this campaign's
#      own attribution run rather than argued (see doc/campaigns/o2-pvo-intercept.md, State).
#   4. `FBMissionRunner` ends the whole run at the first flight-monitor K.O. (`FirstFlightKo`).
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION: IS THE CONTROLLER WORTH THE SAME TO A FORCE THAT HAS ALREADY PAID? The
# brief is correct, the geometry is head-on for the southern pair and a 60 deg crossing for the northern
# one, and the only thing that differs from a fresh force is what step 09 spent and lost. THIS FILE MUST
# THEREFORE BE READ TWICE - once as campaign step 10 and once STANDALONE - and the difference between
# those two runs is the entire measurement. Run standalone it is a fully loaded four-ship; run in the
# campaign it is whatever came home.
#
# THE NIGHT IS DECLARED AND IS MEASURED AT ZERO. O4 priced it at six visual telemetry columns out of 184,
# none of them read by any pilot, and nothing in this tree emits light. It is here because the spec's
# tenth mission is a night exercise and because a declared clock costs nothing; no claim below rests on
# it.
#
# READING RULE (binding): exit 1 or 3 are both expected. The verdict is the PAIR OF READINGS:
#   1. the `campaign CARRY` lines this step receives - which aircraft were dropped, which `set store`
#      lines were deleted, and whether any ground object of the complex is gone,
#   2. the same five readings as sortie 09, taken twice: in the campaign and standalone,
#   3. `campaign ATTRITION` and `campaign EXPENDED` at the end of the campaign log.
# A step that reads identically in both runs would mean the carry moved nothing, and that is a
# publishable answer as long as it is MEASURED and not assumed.
#
# MEASURED (fingerprint 09e71efe2a0791ec as campaign step 10, exit 3): THE MAGAZINE DECIDES THE WEAPON,
# AND THE WEAPON DECIDES THE RANGE. The carry entering this step is 10 `campaign CARRY ... action=stores`
# lines and NOTHING else - `units` and `ground` carried nothing, because wave one lost nobody and the
# complex was never touched. pvo1 enters with 1 R-27R, pvo2 with 0, pvo3 with 0 R-27R and 1 R-73, pvo4
# with 0 AND 0: an empty aeroplane on a correct vector.
#
#   |                    | STANDALONE (full magazines) | AS CAMPAIGN STEP 10 |
#   | releases           | 7  (5 R-27R, 2 R-73)        | 4  (1 R-27R, 3 R-73) |
#   | detonations        | 5                           | 2                    |
#   | first kill         | `w2a` t = 80.6, R-27R, 3.46 m | `w2a` t = 182.3, R-73, 1.37 m |
#   | run length         | 328.1 s                     | 304.6 s              |
#
#   THE SAME FILE, THE SAME BRIEF, THE SAME GEOMETRY, THE SAME CONTROLLER - and the kill moves 101.7 s
#   later and from a semi-active radar round fired at BVR to an infrared round fired at the merge,
#   because that is what was left on the rails. THE CONTROLLER'S WORTH DID NOT CHANGE (both waves take
#   the identical 8.0 s and reach a firm track at the identical 3.0 s after it); WHAT THE FORCE COULD
#   DO WITH HIS VECTOR DID. That is the entire content of this campaign's carry, and it is one
#   difference and not a trend - two points cannot be a curve, and the `.fbc` header says so.
#   `deny release team friendly` MET on all four in both runs; the corridor aircraft `w2c` recovered in
#   both. The night contributed nothing measurable, as declared.

unit pvo1
  team hostile
  flight sok1 1
  module mig29
  spawn 47.30000 48.10000 8000 270.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set n019_mode rad
  set n019_emission off
  set rwr on
  set iff_interrogator on
  set kols_mode ir
  set brief_sort left
  set store 3 r27r
  set store 4 r27r
  set store 5 r73
  set store 6 r73
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task intercept
  set brief_gci 20 270 32 8
  set brief_gci 50 270 18 8
  set brief_gci 75 270 7 8
  wp 47.30000 47.50000 8000 450
  objective kill team friendly
  objective deny release team friendly
  objective survive

